over the last week or so if there's been  this really awesome tweet going around  that recreates the classic iPod using  Swift UI it contains a click wheel that  you can rotate around to change the  position of the album's in the  scrollable view above it and if you've  ever owned an iPod it will definitely  make you feel some nostalgia as soon as  I saw this tweet I knew that I was going  to have to build it in flutter and  that's what this video is all about if  you're new here like and subscribe and  grab the full source code from fire ship  IO let's start by taking a closer look  at the app we're building in this video  now I realize that everybody is sick of  flutter UI challenges but this video is  really all about the UI we're not going  to request access to the user's music or  play actual audio files but there are  plenty of flutter plugins out there if  your goal is to build a full-blown audio  player now if we look at the top of the  UI you'll see that I have a scrollable  page view widget  it focuses an individual item in the  center and then animates it into the  view when it's moving the off-screen  images are given some perspective so  they look 3d and then rotate it the  rotation is calculated based on their  position in the page view and that's how  we get these cool animations when the  squirrel position changes now moving  down to the click wheel we can go from  one item to the next by clicking the  forward or backwards buttons but the  real challenge with this demo was  creating a click wheel that you can pan  across either clockwise or  counterclockwise and have the scroll  view rotate in the proper direction so  when the wheel is rotating clockwise it  should move the page view to the right  when it's moving counterclockwise it  should move the page view back to the  left and we can do all this with just a  regular gesture detector and some basic  math we don't need a custom painter or  trigonometry or anything crazy like that  in fact we don't need a single  dependency for this project so let's go  ahead and jump into a flutter app the  first thing you'll notice is that I have  a list of image URLs to show the album  covers which is completely optional  because those images sit on top of some  colored containers so you can omit them  all together if you want now down in our  main material app we'll go ahead and add  a scaffold and then inside that scaffold  we'll add our iPod widget the first  thing I'll do is show you how to set up  a scrollable page view in our state pool  widget will first instantiate a page  controller and we'll set the viewport  fraction to 0.6 this allows the next and  previous page to overflow into the view  so you get a preview of what's coming  next which is similar to the classic  iPod UI if you have a viewport fraction  of 1 which is the default it will fill  up the entire view the next thing we'll  do is set up a stateful property called  current page which starts at 0 and the  reason we have this as State on the  widget is because we want to update the  UI anytime the current page changes and  there's multiple ways the user can  change that value either by using the  click wheel by pressing a button or by  using the page view directly we can  update that value whenever a change  occurs by adding a listener to the page  controller so whenever the current page  changes it will trigger a rerender on  the component which will be useful once  we get to the animation part now let's  move down to our build method we'll  start with a safe area widget that will  prevent the page view from interfering  with the status bar at the top and  that's actually an optimization I got  from my friend my woolly Joe on Twitter  so thanks for that from there we'll set  up a column and the first item in that  column will be a container with a height  of 300 and a black background the child  of that container will be a page view  builder the page view builder allows you  to render all of the items in the page  view dynamically it's similar to a  virtual scroll feature on the web where  you're only actually rendering the items  that are visible in the view so it can  handle an infinitely large collection  well pass it our page controller then  set the scroll direction to horizontal  then provide an item Builder function  that contains the current index of the  page in the view now because we can have  an infinite number of pages in the page  view I'll go ahead and create a new  stateless widget called an album card it  takes a color as its input along with  the current index and the current page  now you might be wondering why we need  the current page and the current index  because intuitively you might think that  they are the same value and while  they're almost the same value the  current index is an integer while the  current page is a double as you'll see  in just a second we can compare these  two values to determine how far offset  one of the pages is relative to the  center and we can use that relative  position to create some cool 3d  transformations now let's go ahead and  create the album card stateless widget  we'll start by passing in the properties  that we just talked about and we'll  calculate this card's relative position  from the center by subtracting the  current page from the current index if  the value is negative we know the pages  on the left side if it's  we know it's on the right side from  there we'll set up a container with a  child of a transform widget now I could  probably do a whole video on transforms  so don't worry if this part feels a  little overwhelming our transformation  starts as a 4x4 matrix basically this  allows you to take a 3d point in space  and perform other transformations on it  like translation rotation scale and so  on we produce the actual matrix by  calling set entry and the typical  starting arguments that you'll see here  are 3 2 and point 0 0 1 but feel free to  play around with these to get different  effects from there we'll change the  scale or the size of the widget I had to  play around with these values a little  bit but I found it looked best when I  clamped the values to range between  point 6 and one point out so the page in  the centre will be scaled to 1 but the  pages on the sides will be scaled to  around point 6 and lastly we'll add a  transformation to rotate around the  y-axis based on the relative position  and if you want to get really crazy with  things also go ahead and add a rotation  to the z-axis when it comes to the  alignment of the individual pages we  want the items on the right side to be  aligned center left and then the items  on the left side to be aligned center  right in everything below that is just  your basic UI so we have a container  that has a decoration image with a  network image that points to one of the  images in our list if we fire up our app  at this point we should have a page view  that looks something like this when we  pan across the view it cycles between  the albums and animates the  transformations between them now that  that's taken care of we can move on to  the fun part which is building our  radial click wheel it will take the page  controller that we've already built and  then allow the user to pan  either clockwise or counterclockwise to  control the position of the page view  and we'll also add velocity to it as  well so if the user pans faster it will  scroll through the items faster let's  head back up to the column that contains  our page view builder just below it  we'll go ahead and add a centered widget  that contains a child of a stack the  first thing we'll want in the stack is a  gesture detector and the gesture we care  about is on pan update that event will  fire continuously whenever the user is  moving their finger across this  container for now I'm just going to add  a placeholder function there called pan  handler and we'll implement that in just  a minute  then we can create a circle by setting  up a container  with a width and a height then we'll  give it some box decoration where the  shape is box shaped circle now on the  iPod you have more of a doughnut shape  or a circle within a circle and we only  want to fire the pan event when the user  is moving throughout the outside circle  or the black circle if they pan across  the inner grey circle then nothing  should happen because we're inside of a  stack that's aligned to the center we  can simply add another container that  also has a box shape of circle and  because it has a higher index in the  stack it will sit on top of the  gesturedetector and won't allow the  events to get through when it is panned  over now you'll also notice that we have  different buttons inside the wheel  itself  I'm not going to implement all these  only the forward and back buttons these  buttons also sit inside their own stack  and they're positioned relative to the  circles so they appear in the right  locations the ones that are actually  pressable are set up as an icon button  when they're pressed they'll take the  page controller and then animate to the  current page plus one or if we're going  backwards the current page minus one so  now when the user taps one of these  buttons it will animate to the next page  or the previous page and give us a nice  looking animation in the process and  with that we're ready to move on to the  final challenge and that's making our  click wheel rotatable let's start by  making sure we understand how a wheel  works we can break a wheel up into  different quadrants we have the top  right the bottom right the bottom left  and the top left so those are the  different parts of the wheel but then  the user can actually pan with their  finger in four different directions as  well they can go up down left or right  and if we think of this as an XY axis  then we know that different movements or  positions are mutually exclusive like  the user can't be going X positive and X  negative at the same time or in other  words the user can't move left and right  at the same time now our panhandler  method will have access to the drag  update details for every event and that  event contains things like the delta or  the amount of change since the users  last movement as well as the local  position of that users movement on the  widget itself to make our code readable  I'm going to go ahead and set up  variables for all these different values  since our circle widget has a fixed  width of 300 pixels we'll go ahead and  set up a radius value of 150 we can  determine where the user is dragging by  looking at the local position  the user is on the top half of the  circle if the local position on the  y-axis is less than or equal to the  radius we know they're on the left side  if the local position x-axis is less  than or equal to the radius now we know  if the user is on top then they can't be  on bottom or if they're on the left side  that can't be on the right side so we'll  grab those values by simply grabbing the  opposite other counterpart to determine  the direction of the users movement we  can look at the Delta on the event Delta  is just a fancy word for change it  represents the amount of movement that  the user has made since the last event  in this case we know that they're moving  up if the Y movement is less than or  equal to 0 or they're moving left if the  X movement is less than or equal to 0  and then we can use the bang to get  their opposites just for the sake of  making our code a little more readable  now that we can pinpoint exactly how and  where a user is panning around we'll go  ahead and set up values for the X change  and wide change as their absolute values  and now we need to make this value  negative or positive depending on which  way they're rotating the wheel let's  start by thinking about how vertical  movement should change the rotation of  the wheel if they're on the right side  and panning down or on the left side and  panning up then that white change should  be positive or clockwise otherwise it'll  be negative or counterclockwise so we  just take the total change and multiply  it by a negative 1 now we'll do the same  thing for the horizontal rotation if  we're on top and panning right or on the  bottom and panning left then the change  is clockwise otherwise counter clockwise  so to get the total rotation value all  we have to do is add these two values  together it's also a good idea to add  velocity so if the user is panning  faster it will scroll through the items  faster and we can do that by multiplying  by the Delta distance and now that we  have all the data we need we just need  to use our page controller to jump to a  new position and that new position is  the current page controller offset plus  the scroll offset change that we just  calculated and that's all there is to it  you can now use the same logic to  implement a rotatable knob or some kind  of wheel or any other type of radial UI  widget that the user has control over  I'm gonna go ahead and wrap things up  there if this video helped you please  like and subscribe and consider becoming  a pro member at fire ship IO to get  access to the full flutter' course along  with a bunch of other advanced content  for members only thanks for watching and  we'll talk to you soon  [Music]

